‘The state should be allowed to remove deliberate false reports because they cause panic and real harm – like the fictional drinking water alert that led to panic buying and overloaded emergency phone lines.’ {{1}}
‘Removing content is simply right; everyone knows that.’ {{2}}
‘The state should not be allowed to remove content because what is true often only becomes clear later – a report removed too hastily may turn out to be partly true.’ {{3}}
‘Anyone who opposes removal does not care about the truth.’ {{4}}
‘Giving the state the power to remove content is dangerous because it can be turned against critics – authoritarian systems regularly justify their censorship as a fight against false information.’ {{5}}
‘Someone should finally give the internet a proper clean-up.’ {{6}}
‘Removing content protects informed opinion because otherwise voters decide on fabrications rather than the issue itself – for example, when a made-up report about secret plans circulates before a local vote.’ {{7}}
‘Removing content can have the opposite effect because content that has been taken down gets even more attention – the Streisand effect has shown this since 2003.’ {{8}}
‘The state should be allowed to remove content because made-up claims can ruin lives and corrections never reach everyone – as with the fictional restaurant falsely accused of serving rotten meat.’ {{9}}
‘Freedom of expression is more important anyway, end of story.’ {{10}}
